{"_id":"wmPlayerGuide001","name":"Player Guide","ownership":{"default":2},"pages":[{"_id":"wmGuidePage00001","name":"Welcome & quick reference","type":"text","sort":100000,"ownership":{"default":2},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>Welcome &amp; quick reference</h1><p>Player guide</p></header><p>Use this guide for crafting, professions, equipment and exploration. The Workbench and character sheets show your campaign’s configured values.</p><ul><li>Choose one retrainable profession and use its Lore.</li><li>Craft, enchant, gather and disassemble through the Workbench.</li><li>Assign up to six artisans to earn teamwork bonuses.</li><li>Use the party Exploration tab for travel and shared activities.</li></ul><p>Artisan Marks are retired. Current crafting uses materials, native property runes, Crafting Edges and Masterstrokes.</p></section>"}},{"_id":"wmGuidePage00002","name":"Professions","type":"text","sort":200000,"ownership":{"default":2},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>Professions</h1><p>Player guide</p></header><p>Choose one profession and retrain it into another at any time. It grants the named Lore and Additional Lore, with trained proficiency at level 1, expert at 3, master at 7 and legendary at 15. Later levels do not grant additional professions.</p><p>Relevant profession checks retain a +2 circumstance bonus; it does not stack with another circumstance bonus. A relevant profession Lore is offered first for crafting and gathering. You may select another eligible skill.</p><h2>Core professions</h2><ul><li>Blacksmithing</li><li>Alchemy</li><li>Enchanting</li><li>Leatherwork</li><li>Carpentry</li><li>Stonemasonry</li><li>Glassmaking</li><li>Pottery</li><li>Weaving</li><li>Bookmaking</li><li>Tailoring</li></ul><p>The core profession grants no additional bonus feats beyond Additional Lore.</p></section>"}},{"_id":"wmGuidePage00003","name":"Hero & Nephilim Points","type":"text","sort":300000,"ownership":{"default":2},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>Hero &amp; Nephilim Points</h1><p>Player guide</p></header><h2>Hero Points</h2><p>You can hold up to 10 Hero Points. Unspent points carry over between sessions. Use the normal character-sheet control; ordinary Hero Point spending rules still apply unless your GM says otherwise.</p><h2>Nephilim Bonds</h2><p>Open the three-dot menu beside Nephilim Bonds on the character sheet. Choose bonds at levels 5, 10, 15 and 20; unchosen earlier slots remain available. The control highlights when a choice is due.</p><h2>Nephilim Points</h2><p>The party has a separate shared tracker from 0 to 10 in its header. These points persist with the party. The GM determines when the party earns or spends these points and what benefits they provide.</p></section>"}},{"_id":"wmGuidePage00004","name":"Equipment, Core materials & Apex items","type":"text","sort":400000,"ownership":{"default":2},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>Equipment, Core materials &amp; Apex items</h1><p>Player guide</p></header><p>A crafted item combines a PF2e base item with a Core material. Weapon materials grant attack bonuses; only Striking runes increase base weapon damage dice.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Weapon attack</th><th>Spell attack / DC</th><th>Armor AC / saves</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>+0</td><td>+0</td><td>+0 / +0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>+1</td><td>+1</td><td>+1 / +1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>+2</td><td>+2</td><td>+2 / +2</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>+3</td><td>+3</td><td>+3 / +3</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>+4</td><td>+4</td><td>+4 / +4</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>+5</td><td>+5</td><td>+5 / +5</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Materials do not grant weapon damage dice. Shields gain +3 Hardness and +30 HP per Core progression step. The displayed item includes applicable modifications.</p><h2>Omnipotentium</h2><p>Omni Iron, Omni Steel, Creation Steel, Fate Steel, Eternal Steel and Iron’s Blood are Tiers 1–6 of an alternative Core family for weapons and armour. Any tier bypasses Voidborn protection: other weapons and spells deal half damage to Voidborn creatures. Equipped Omni armour halves damage from Voidborn attackers. An active focus with an Omni frame also bypasses protection.</p><p>Omnipotentium is awarded only by the GM. It cannot be gathered and has no standard market or sale value. Find its ingots in their dedicated compendium; Void Protection has its own compendium.</p><h2>Spell Focus</h2><p>A held or worn focus with a Mana Gem Core grants its Core spellcasting bonus to spell attacks and DCs, not spell damage, slots or proficiency. Only the strongest active focus applies.</p><h2>Dragon Scales</h2><p>Scales enhance Metal or Leather/Hide armor. Hatchling, Juvenile, Youth, Adult, Ancient and Arch Dragon grant default resistance values 1, 4, 8, 12, 16 and 20. Black grants acid, Blue electricity, Green poison, Red fire and White cold resistance while the armor is equipped.</p><p>Scale quantities: buckler/small shield 1, light shield 2, standard shield 3, tower shield 4, fortress/oversized shield 5; clothing/light armour 2, medium 3, heavy 4, full plate/oversized heavy 5.</p><h2>Equipment size</h2><p>Medium is the default. Resource multipliers are Tiny ×0.5, Small ×0.75, Medium ×1, Large ×1.25, Huge ×1.5 and Gargantuan ×1.75. Round down, minimum one unit per requirement. Property runes always cost one Mana Gem each.</p><h2>Apex ability equipment</h2><p>These 30 worn items grant +1 to +5 to Strength, Dexterity, Constitution, Intelligence, Wisdom or Charisma. Wear and invest the item, then use its circled A on the character sheet. Different attributes can be active together; for the same attribute only the strongest applies. Belts, rings, amulets, circlets, sashes and brooches form the collection. These are campaign rewards, not a priced shop list.</p></section>"}},{"_id":"wmGuidePage00005","name":"Resource catalogue","type":"text","sort":500000,"ownership":{"default":2},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>Resource catalogue</h1><p>Player guide</p></header><p>Resources are Equipment with the Precious tag. Each inventory unit is one Resource Unit, including named lots of crystals or scales. Five units weigh 1 Bulk; ten weigh 2 Bulk. Prices below are full unit values, before a sale discount. Existing older inventory copies may still appear as Treasure.</p><p>Tier levels are 1, 4, 8, 12, 16 and 20, with default level-based DCs 15, 19, 24, 30, 35 and 40. The actual attempt may have an adjustment.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Tier</th><th>Level</th><th>Unit value</th><th>Unit Bulk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Iron Ingot</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>3 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Steel Ingot</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>15 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Cold Iron Ingot</td><td>3</td><td>8</td><td>85 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Mithril Ingot</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>350 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Adamantium Ingot</td><td>5</td><td>16</td><td>1600 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Dark Iron Ingot</td><td>6</td><td>20</td><td>11000 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Softwood Lumber</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>3 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Hardwood Lumber</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>15 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Blackwood Lumber</td><td>3</td><td>8</td><td>85 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Darkmoon Lumber</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>350 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Starwood Lumber</td><td>5</td><td>16</td><td>1600 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Godwood Lumber</td><td>6</td><td>20</td><td>11000 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Fieldstone Block</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>3 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Granite Block</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>15 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Obsidian Block</td><td>3</td><td>8</td><td>85 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Runestone Block</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>350 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Celestite Block</td><td>5</td><td>16</td><td>1600 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Worldstone Block</td><td>6</td><td>20</td><td>11000 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Rawhide Sheet</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>3 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Hardened Leather Sheet</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>15 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Ironhide Sheet</td><td>3</td><td>8</td><td>85 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Moonhide Sheet</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>350 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Titanhide Sheet</td><td>5</td><td>16</td><td>1600 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Primordial Hide Sheet</td><td>6</td><td>20</td><td>11000 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Greenleaf Bundle</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>3 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Embercap Bundle</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>15 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Ghostmoss Bundle</td><td>3</td><td>8</td><td>85 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Moonbloom Bundle</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>350 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Starspore Bundle</td><td>5</td><td>16</td><td>1600 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Worldroot Bundle</td><td>6</td><td>20</td><td>11000 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Faint Mana Crystals</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>3 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Charged Mana Crystals</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>15 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Resonant Mana Crystals</td><td>3</td><td>8</td><td>85 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Arcane Prisms</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>350 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Astral Crystals</td><td>5</td><td>16</td><td>1600 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Aetherheart Crystals</td><td>6</td><td>20</td><td>11000 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Hatchling Black Dragon Scales</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>4.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Juvenile Black Dragon Scales</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>22.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Youth Black Dragon Scales</td><td>3</td><td>8</td><td>127.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Adult Black Dragon Scales</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>525 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Ancient Black Dragon Scales</td><td>5</td><td>16</td><td>2400 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Arch Dragon Black Dragon Scales</td><td>6</td><td>20</td><td>16500 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Hatchling Blue Dragon Scales</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>4.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Juvenile Blue Dragon Scales</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>22.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Youth Blue Dragon Scales</td><td>3</td><td>8</td><td>127.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Adult Blue Dragon Scales</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>525 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Ancient Blue Dragon Scales</td><td>5</td><td>16</td><td>2400 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Arch Dragon Blue Dragon Scales</td><td>6</td><td>20</td><td>16500 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Hatchling Green Dragon Scales</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>4.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Juvenile Green Dragon Scales</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>22.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Youth Green Dragon Scales</td><td>3</td><td>8</td><td>127.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Adult Green Dragon Scales</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>525 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Ancient Green Dragon Scales</td><td>5</td><td>16</td><td>2400 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Arch Dragon Green Dragon Scales</td><td>6</td><td>20</td><td>16500 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Hatchling Red Dragon Scales</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>4.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Juvenile Red Dragon Scales</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>22.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Youth Red Dragon Scales</td><td>3</td><td>8</td><td>127.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Adult Red Dragon Scales</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>525 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Ancient Red Dragon Scales</td><td>5</td><td>16</td><td>2400 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Arch Dragon Red Dragon Scales</td><td>6</td><td>20</td><td>16500 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Hatchling White Dragon Scales</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>4.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Juvenile White Dragon Scales</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>22.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Youth White Dragon Scales</td><td>3</td><td>8</td><td>127.5 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Adult White Dragon Scales</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>525 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Ancient White Dragon Scales</td><td>5</td><td>16</td><td>2400 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Arch Dragon White Dragon Scales</td><td>6</td><td>20</td><td>16500 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Omni Iron Ingots</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Omni Steel Ingots</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>0 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Creation Steel Ingots</td><td>3</td><td>8</td><td>0 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Fate Steel Ingots</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>0 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Eternal Steel Ingots</td><td>5</td><td>16</td><td>0 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr><tr><td>Iron&#39;s Blood Ingots</td><td>6</td><td>20</td><td>0 gp</td><td>0.2</td></tr></tbody></table></section>"}},{"_id":"wmGuidePage00006","name":"Crafting in the Workbench","type":"text","sort":600000,"ownership":{"default":2},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>Crafting in the Workbench</h1><p>Player guide</p></header><ol><li>Choose the party, drop a supported base item into Craft, and select its recipe, Core material and Tier.</li><li>Add one lead artisan. Ordinary recipes can be crafted solo; selected enhancements may require an additional qualified artisan. Additional artisans contribute to the work. Dragon-scale reinforcement requires a Leatherwork artisan. </li><li>Review materials and days required.</li><li>Create the project to reserve Party Stash stock. Reservations prevent promising the same stock to multiple projects; they do not consume it.</li><li>Commit downtime and resolve Work Blocks. At completion, review and confirm the resource consumption and finished item.</li></ol><h2>Working together</h2><p>Up to six distinct artisans contribute. Each artisan after the lead grants +1 to Work Block rolls, up to +5. This stacks with profession bonuses and the existing teamwork time reduction. Each spends the same calendar downtime. Teamwork work rates are ×1 for 1–2 artisans, ×1.25 for 3–5, and ×1.5 for six. Fractional Progress carries between blocks. For a block of D days (1–5), the base success gives D Progress; critical success rolls a Crafting Edge (normally ceiling(1.5 × D)); failure gives zero for one day or floor(D ÷ 2) for longer blocks; critical failure gives zero. Apply the teamwork rate to that base Progress. Time estimates assume success.</p><h2>Crafting Edges</h2>\n<p>Any critical success on a Work Block automatically rolls a Crafting Edge. Roll a d3 unless the block’s accelerated work could complete the contribution; then roll a d4. Each project is one shared contribution. Apply the existing teamwork rate after the Edge’s base work. If result 4 cannot finish at 100% work, it becomes Accelerated Work and does not roll a Masterstroke.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>d3 / d4</th><th>Result</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Accelerated Work</td><td>Generate 150% of committed days as work, rounded up. No additional effect.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Material Conservation</td><td>Generate 100% work. Preserve workshop consumables worth up to 10% of one selected Resource Unit used by this contribution. This credit cannot be sold or converted into fractional Resource Units.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Stable Integration</td><td>Generate 150% work, rounded up. Gain a +2 circumstance bonus to the next Work Block for this contribution.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Masterstroke Opportunity</td><td>Generate 100% work. If this completes the contribution, roll a Masterstroke; otherwise, use Accelerated Work.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Conservation credits remain in the project details, including the archive. Select the resource when the credit is gained; closing the selection uses the first listed resource. Apply up to the recorded value to workshop consumables and select Use Credit to expend it. Credits never create stash materials or currency.</p>\n<h2>Masterstrokes</h2><p>A completed Masterstroke Opportunity automatically rolls a d8. Masterstrokes appear in the project, chat readout, and finished item’s Masterstroke controls. Hidden Detail is permitted on all crafted equipment.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>d8</th><th>Result</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Maker’s Signature</td><td>A harmless visual, audible, or tactile maker motif appears when the item is readied or activated. While its provenance remains intact, the item can be authenticated as that artisan’s work.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Resilient Finish</td><td>Gain a +2 circumstance bonus to the first future Repair check made on this item, then expend this benefit.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Opened Channel</td><td>Gain a +2 circumstance bonus to the first Work Block check of this item’s next upgrade project, then expend this benefit.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Efficient Maintenance</td><td>The first future repair or maintenance contribution requires 1 fewer artisan-day, to a minimum of 1, then expend this benefit.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Hidden Detail</td><td>The item can conceal one object of negligible Bulk. Finding it requires a Search or Perception check against the item’s current Crafting DC.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Balanced Carry</td><td>While carried or stowed, the item counts as one Bulk step lighter, to a minimum of negligible. This does not alter wielding, usage, armor category, damage, value, or recipe requirements.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Resonant Tell</td><td>The current bearer receives a harmless sensory warning when the item becomes damaged, one of its Marks becomes Dormant, or its tracked charges are exhausted.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Storied Presence</td><td>Gain a +1 circumstance bonus to Make an Impression or Request when this item’s documented maker or provenance is directly relevant. This does not stack with another circumstance bonus.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3>Using benefits</h3><p>Stable Integration applies automatically to the next Work Block. Opened Channel applies automatically to the first check of the next upgrade, then is expended even on a failed check. Circumstance bonuses do not stack. Balanced Carry adjusts carried or stowed Bulk; it does not reduce worn armor or held weapon Bulk. Resonant Tell privately notifies the bearer’s owners when recorded damage or exhausted charges change.</p>\n<p>Use Roll Repair (+2) on the item for Resilient Finish; resolve repaired Hit Points or damage normally. If the benefit is used with another Repair workflow, apply the circumstance bonus there and select Mark Used on the item. Use Maintenance Benefit calculates the one-day reduction and records its expenditure. Roll Diplomacy (+1) prompts for Make an Impression or Request; use it only when the maker or provenance is relevant. Maker’s Signature and Hidden Detail provide narrative benefits; the concealed object is tracked normally by the players, and the item shows its current Crafting DC.</p><p>Cancel a project to release unspent reservations. Archive finished projects to keep the list manageable. Deleting a record does not refund spent materials. Report a missing finished item to the GM rather than crafting a duplicate.</p></section>"}},{"_id":"wmGuidePage00007","name":"Enchanting equipment","type":"text","sort":700000,"ownership":{"default":2},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>Enchanting equipment</h1><p>Player guide</p></header><h2>Enchant while crafting</h2>\n<p>Assign an Enchanting artisan to an Assistant Artisan slot, then click Enchant Item on their card. Choose a Mana Gem tier and an eligible property rune. The rune list shows descriptions; bookmarks open the full item reference. Planned runes appear in the yellow panel beneath the crafting controls. Remove a rune there to change the plan.</p>\n<p>Each rune reserves one Mana Gem of the selected tier with the project. Gems are consumed and native rune effects applied when the item is completed. The enchanter counts toward teamwork. Removing the enchanter or selecting incompatible equipment prevents the enchanted project from starting.</p>\n<h2>Enchant finished equipment</h2><p>Use Enchant at the top of the Workbench. Choose an Enchanting artisan you control, a single weapon or suit of armour, and an unreserved Mana Gem from the Party Stash. Select a rune and confirm to consume one unit immediately and apply the rune. An active GM handles shared-stash requests. Split equipment stacks first and finish any active upgrade before enchanting.</p>\n<h2>Rune limits and costs</h2><p>Mana Gem tiers 1–6 allow rune levels up to 1, 4, 8, 12, 16 and 20 respectively. Each property rune costs one resource unit, regardless of equipment size. Native equipment restrictions apply. Material attack or armour bonuses supply rune slots, capped at three; no potency rune is required. With the default progression, tiers 1–6 provide 0, 1, 2, 3, 3 and 3 slots. Improving an existing rune replaces its lower-grade version. Striking runes remain responsible for extra base weapon damage dice.</p></section>"}},{"_id":"wmGuidePage00008","name":"Gathering, upgrades & disassembly","type":"text","sort":800000,"ownership":{"default":2},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>Gathering, upgrades &amp; disassembly</h1><p>Player guide</p></header><h2>Gather</h2><p>Use the Workbench Gather tab. Select a character, environment and resource; review its Tier, skill, DC, time and expected yield, then attempt the check. Region and environment can restrict available goods. One lead character rolls. On a success, add one material per party character to the Party Stash; on a critical success, add two per character. You can use relevant profession Lore instead of the listed skill.</p><h2>Upgrade</h2><p>Stow and uninvest a single crafted item, then drop it into Upgrade. Choose a new Core material or Tier, secondary materials and Tiers, or dragon scales. Legacy Mark records remain for historical reference and grant no benefits.</p><p>Replacement components require 25% fewer materials, rounded up per requirement, and 25% less component work. Unchanged components cost nothing. Old replaced materials are lost. Reservations and artisan teamwork follow normal projects; completion consumes the replacements and updates the original item, not a copy. Cancel to release the item and resources. Reserved items cannot be edited, equipped or dismantled. Use the latest project for recovery or disassembly.</p><p>Dragon-scale Resource Units cost 1.5 times the normal tier value. Mana crystals use normal tier prices.</p><h2>Disassemble</h2><p>Drop an item with a Core material and tier from your sheet or Party Stash into Disassemble. Review returns before confirming: the item is destroyed. Default recovery is 50%, rounded up separately for each material, Tier and variant. Recorded items use their project ledger; other compatible items show a standard-recipe estimate. Returns go to the selected Party Stash. Dismantled items cannot be salvaged twice or recovered as missing outputs.</p></section>"}},{"_id":"wmGuidePage00009","name":"Flanking","type":"text","sort":900000,"ownership":{"default":2},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>Flanking</h1><p>Player guide</p></header><p>First establish a normal opposite-side flank with two qualifying melee combatants in reach. Additional qualifying combatants can contribute from other sides.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Qualifying combatants</th><th>Final target AC penalty</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2</td><td>−2: Off-guard (Flanked)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>−3: Outnumbered (Flanked)</td></tr><tr><td>4 or more</td><td>−4: Surrounded (Flanked)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are final penalties, not additions to the normal −2. Only qualifying melee attacks benefit; ranged attacks do not inherit this flanking penalty. Reach weapons count when they can reach the target. A creature unable to flank or an immune target follows its normal restrictions.</p><p>If the target is more than one size category larger than the largest qualifying flanker, the enhanced thresholds become six for −3 and eight for −4. A normal two-person flank still gives −2.</p></section>"}},{"_id":"wmGuidePage00010","name":"Party travel & exploration","type":"text","sort":1000000,"ownership":{"default":2},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>Party travel &amp; exploration</h1><p>Player guide</p></header><p>The party's Exploration tab combines normal exploration activities with travel setup, rider assignments and the daily plan. On foot, the slowest member limits shared Speed. Vehicle riders use transport Speed, while walkers can still slow the group. Pulled transport uses the slowest selected hauler, subject to the vehicle limit.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Shared Speed</th><th>Activities per day</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10 feet or less</td><td>½ (one activity over two days)</td></tr><tr><td>15–25 feet</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>30–40 feet</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>45–55 feet</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>60 feet or more</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Assign Travel, Reconnoiter, Fortify Camp, Map the Area, Subsist or Other and mark activities Used as they finish. Save Travel Plan preserves choices; Begin Day & Share posts the plan. The sun button starts a new day.</p><h2>Express Rider house rule</h2><p>Select the character rolling Nature, pulling creatures and up to six affected party travellers, including walkers. The check uses the highest selected Will DC. Success or critical success increases affected overland Speeds by half for that day. Unaffected walkers and vehicle limits still constrain the group. This changes overland travel, not encounter Speed.</p></section>"}}]}
